Happiness is universally pursued for its own sake, indicating it is an end in itself rather than a means to another goal.
Aristotle's concept of eudaimonia emphasizes that true happiness comes from living virtuously, not from external achievements.
Even if external factors contribute to happiness, the core experience of happiness is internal and self-sufficient.
Psychological studies show that long-term happiness is more closely linked to internal states than to material success.
If happiness depended on external factors, it would be unstable and unreliable, undermining its value as a fundamental good.